1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,530

2
00:00:05,530 --> 00:00:07,863
So today I'm going to be
talking about some recent work

3
00:00:07,863 --> 00:00:09,280
that we've been
doing at DeepMind,

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:11,470
developing this line
of architectures

5
00:00:11,470 --> 00:00:13,750
that we're calling perceivers.

6
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:17,060
And I'll be motivating this in
terms of a goal that we have,

7
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:20,270
which is to develop a general
purpose architectures.

8
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:22,330
And so just right
off the bat, I want

9
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:25,670
to motivate why we care about
general purpose architectures.

10
00:00:25,670 --> 00:00:27,700
And so the first--

11
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:29,890
both of the reasons
are fairly pragmatic.

12
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:32,259
But basically the
idea is if we're

13
00:00:32,259 --> 00:00:34,750
thinking about all of the
data that we could possibly

14
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:38,117
imagine collecting in
the world, a lot of it

15
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:39,700
is basically involved
in what we think

16
00:00:39,700 --> 00:00:42,560
of as sort of traditional
sense modalities.

17
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,130
And these things range from
touch, and proprioception,

18
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:47,650
to echolocation, to
the kind of perception

19
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:49,820
you need to ingest texts--

20
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:51,910
however you want
to format that--

21
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:56,290
just to more exotic things
like event-based cameras,

22
00:00:56,290 --> 00:00:59,350
touching with your senses--
things like smell and depth--

23
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:02,710
and all the way up to the
kinds of sense modalities

24
00:01:02,710 --> 00:01:04,629
that we really think
about when we're thinking

25
00:01:04,629 --> 00:01:06,580
about scientific perception.

26
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:10,270
And so basically if we think
about the full set of data

27
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:12,040
and what it would
take to actually model

28
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:14,230
each of these
different modalities,

29
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:17,342
it basically looks
effectively intractable to try

30
00:01:17,342 --> 00:01:19,300
to the engineer inductive
biases that will work

31
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,020
for every single one of these.

32
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:24,003
So we don't want to
engineer them one by one.

33
00:01:24,003 --> 00:01:25,420
This is an approach
that's worked.

34
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:27,700
And in some ways it's maybe
a reasonable description

35
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,160
of how we think about
developing new architectures

36
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:31,900
for different problems.

37
00:01:31,900 --> 00:01:33,320
But it's just not
going to scale.

38
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:36,713
We can't afford as a
community to hand design

39
00:01:36,713 --> 00:01:38,380
inductive biases that
will work for each

40
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:39,290
and every one of these.

41
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:40,450
And so rather than
doing that, we

42
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:42,040
want to sort of
build architectures

43
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:45,733
that at least a first pass will
allow us to handle everything.

44
00:01:45,733 --> 00:01:47,150
There's another
practical argument

45
00:01:47,150 --> 00:01:50,293
for why we should work on
general purpose architectures.

46
00:01:50,293 --> 00:01:52,460
And that's because it will
allow us to build simpler

47
00:01:52,460 --> 00:01:55,290
and more unified systems.

48
00:01:55,290 --> 00:01:57,290
So if you look at
how, in particular,

49
00:01:57,290 --> 00:02:01,310
complex multimodal data
streams are typically

50
00:02:01,310 --> 00:02:06,470
approached in the sensory
computer vision or pattern

51
00:02:06,470 --> 00:02:08,330
recognition literatures.

52
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:10,130
Effectively, the
typical way this is done

53
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:12,230
is by using inductive
biases that we now

54
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:14,490
hold for the
individual modalities,

55
00:02:14,490 --> 00:02:16,730
and then engineer
ways of combining

56
00:02:16,730 --> 00:02:18,140
those different subsystems.

57
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:22,915
So this can mean building
specific heads, specific input

58
00:02:22,915 --> 00:02:24,290
modules for each
of these things,

59
00:02:24,290 --> 00:02:26,207
and then trying out the
various different ways

60
00:02:26,207 --> 00:02:27,570
of combining them.

61
00:02:27,570 --> 00:02:29,690
So this can work,
but it gives us

62
00:02:29,690 --> 00:02:32,360
systems that in principle
really will only work on one

63
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:34,010
or a small number of domains.

64
00:02:34,010 --> 00:02:36,410
And it gives us systems that
are very hard to maintain,

65
00:02:36,410 --> 00:02:38,287
tend to be fragile,
tend to depend

66
00:02:38,287 --> 00:02:40,370
on specific processing
assumptions about the input

67
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:41,730
modalities.

68
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:43,520
So rather than do
that, we sort of

69
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:45,440
want to move in a
direction of having

70
00:02:45,440 --> 00:02:48,800
unified black-box architectures
that kind of just work.

71
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:51,330
And the idea here is that
if we can get to that point,

72
00:02:51,330 --> 00:02:54,470
we can abstract the architecture
construction process

73
00:02:54,470 --> 00:02:56,930
and really focus on other
more high-level problems.

74
00:02:56,930 --> 00:02:59,090
So this is sort
of the motivation

75
00:02:59,090 --> 00:03:01,270
for this line of work.

76
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:03,490
And the way that we're
going to be doing this

77
00:03:03,490 --> 00:03:06,910
is of course by working on
the most general purpose

78
00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:08,720
architecture that
we have so far,

79
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:10,240
which is basically
a transformer.

80
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:12,580
And you'll all be very familiar
with the basic building

81
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:14,320
blocks of a transformer.

82
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:16,120
But just at a very
high level, we

83
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:17,860
can think about
what they do right,

84
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:21,070
which is they use a general
purpose inductive bias.

85
00:03:21,070 --> 00:03:22,445
So they're non-local,
which means

86
00:03:22,445 --> 00:03:24,362
they're not making domain
specific assumptions

87
00:03:24,362 --> 00:03:26,500
about which point should
be compared to each other,

88
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:29,770
rather they tend to be global in
terms of the attentional focus

89
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,626
that they have.

90
00:03:31,626 --> 00:03:35,270
They use a position as a feature
rather than a hard constraint

91
00:03:35,270 --> 00:03:36,170
of the architecture.

92
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:40,280
And this is in contrast to
sort of NLP-based architectures

93
00:03:40,280 --> 00:03:43,640
or ConvNets in the way
that they typically work,

94
00:03:43,640 --> 00:03:46,610
which used position as an
architectural component

95
00:03:46,610 --> 00:03:50,180
to constrain how
compute is happening.

96
00:03:50,180 --> 00:03:52,490
And then of course
finally, there's

97
00:03:52,490 --> 00:03:56,450
extensive weight sharing in the
way that that they're designed.

98
00:03:56,450 --> 00:03:58,250
And because they
focus on Matmuls,

99
00:03:58,250 --> 00:03:59,840
they tend to be TPU
and GPU friendly.

100
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:01,798
So these are all very
nice things about the way

101
00:04:01,798 --> 00:04:03,475
transformers work.

102
00:04:03,475 --> 00:04:04,850
Of course on the
other hand, they

103
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:07,220
have very poor compute
memory scaling.

104
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:09,030
And there are two
components to this.

105
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:11,420
So attention itself
scales quadratically.

106
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:17,060
So there's this O of
M squared L complexity

107
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:20,060
at the heart of transformers,
and I like writing it this way,

108
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:23,240
because it really emphasizes
that this is a property of--

109
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,100
basically as you make
your models bigger,

110
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:28,160
either at the input size
or as you make them deeper,

111
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:30,800
this problem is just
going to get worse.

112
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,245
And because you have this
scaling in depth as well,

113
00:04:34,245 --> 00:04:35,870
there's another sort
of practical thing

114
00:04:35,870 --> 00:04:39,125
that happens here because
the amount of compute

115
00:04:39,125 --> 00:04:41,250
that we're doing is
proportional to the input size.

116
00:04:41,250 --> 00:04:42,920
So there's no
bottleneck in the way

117
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:45,170
that standard transformers work.

118
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:48,570
Even the linear scaling
becomes a problem.

119
00:04:48,570 --> 00:04:52,010
And so in practice for very,
very large transformers,

120
00:04:52,010 --> 00:04:54,240
this can often be the
bottleneck that really matters.

121
00:04:54,240 --> 00:04:55,490
But they're both at play here.

122
00:04:55,490 --> 00:04:58,590
And so we really want to sort
of tamp down both of these.

123
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:00,200
And so the sort of
perspective here

124
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:02,660
is that to have really
general purpose architectures,

125
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:05,200
we can't have ones that are
just in principle general.

126
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:06,950
We have to have ones
that you can actually

127
00:05:06,950 --> 00:05:10,340
use on the scales and the kinds
of data that we care about.

128
00:05:10,340 --> 00:05:13,250

129
00:05:13,250 --> 00:05:14,990
And so just to--

130
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:16,730
this will all be old
hat for all of you,

131
00:05:16,730 --> 00:05:19,220
but just the way that
standard QKV attention

132
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:21,180
works is basically like this.

133
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:23,240
So it's all about
matrix multiplications.

134
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:24,320
So we have some input.

135
00:05:24,320 --> 00:05:26,750
We compute the query
keys and values

136
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:29,330
by having a 1d convolution,
a 1 by 1 convolution

137
00:05:29,330 --> 00:05:31,260
that we run over the input.

138
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:34,800
We then compute the
attention scores.

139
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,190
This is a matrix multiply
that has the following,

140
00:05:37,190 --> 00:05:38,690
these sorts of shapes.

141
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:42,470
We then use the
output here to compute

142
00:05:42,470 --> 00:05:45,530
the weights to compute the
actual output of the attention

143
00:05:45,530 --> 00:05:46,670
module itself.

144
00:05:46,670 --> 00:05:49,760
And then finally, we run this
through an additional MLP,

145
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:53,450
which is applied convolutionally
to get the outputs.

146
00:05:53,450 --> 00:05:57,930
So this is the starting point
of what we're working on here.

147
00:05:57,930 --> 00:06:00,320
And let me just
briefly just reiterate

148
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:02,600
why we would want the
sort of advantages

149
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:04,910
that we have with these sort
of standard transformers.

150
00:06:04,910 --> 00:06:08,510
So non-locality is one of the
two inductive bias principles

151
00:06:08,510 --> 00:06:09,477
that we have here.

152
00:06:09,477 --> 00:06:11,060
It's useful, I think,
to contrast this

153
00:06:11,060 --> 00:06:13,130
to way that the
effective locality

154
00:06:13,130 --> 00:06:16,110
that you get in convnets and
what this actually means.

155
00:06:16,110 --> 00:06:20,660
So if we look at basically as a
function of depth, which inputs

156
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:22,220
can see which other
ones, which means

157
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:26,498
like how easily it is to express
a function of two input points.

158
00:06:26,498 --> 00:06:28,790
Let's say we look at these
this yellow and purple point

159
00:06:28,790 --> 00:06:29,690
here at the input.

160
00:06:29,690 --> 00:06:32,450
Now I set them sort of
as far apart as possible.

161
00:06:32,450 --> 00:06:36,680
But we might ask how deep
would the effect of computation

162
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:39,170
have to be before you
actually process these two?

163
00:06:39,170 --> 00:06:43,667
And if you look at a 3 by 3
convolution, you're talking--

164
00:06:43,667 --> 00:06:45,750
you're having to look
basically until the very end

165
00:06:45,750 --> 00:06:48,840
of the network until
you're processing

166
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:50,220
these things together.

167
00:06:50,220 --> 00:06:53,490
And what this means
is that the functions

168
00:06:53,490 --> 00:06:55,327
that you can express
that actually

169
00:06:55,327 --> 00:06:57,660
look at both of these points
end up being quite shallow,

170
00:06:57,660 --> 00:06:59,190
because they have
to be built on top

171
00:06:59,190 --> 00:07:01,232
of this very, very deep
stack that just gives you

172
00:07:01,232 --> 00:07:02,860
the locality.

173
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:05,530
And so in point of
fact, if you look at,

174
00:07:05,530 --> 00:07:07,110
for example, the
way ResNets work,

175
00:07:07,110 --> 00:07:09,930
so you have an initial block
which has a 7 by 7 convolution.

176
00:07:09,930 --> 00:07:12,450
And then afterwards it's 3
by 3 columns all the way up.

177
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:16,080
You need 28, 3 by 3 convs
with that standard processing

178
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:19,560
stack before all of the 224
by 224 pixels in an image

179
00:07:19,560 --> 00:07:20,940
you're looking at each other.

180
00:07:20,940 --> 00:07:24,030
And what this means is
that in a ResNet 50,

181
00:07:24,030 --> 00:07:25,830
the points on the very
edge of the pixels

182
00:07:25,830 --> 00:07:27,750
actually never see each other.

183
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:29,438
And it's a little bit--

184
00:07:29,438 --> 00:07:31,230
I found this a little
bit counterintuitive.

185
00:07:31,230 --> 00:07:33,485
But it suggests that we
really are constraining

186
00:07:33,485 --> 00:07:35,610
quite a lot, the functions
that are easy to express

187
00:07:35,610 --> 00:07:36,580
with these models.

188
00:07:36,580 --> 00:07:38,288
And so there are some
functions of images

189
00:07:38,288 --> 00:07:42,700
you just can't capture
with a ResNet 50.

190
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,950
On the other hand, if you
look at an architecture that

191
00:07:44,950 --> 00:07:48,280
has global attention
over the full input, so

192
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:50,350
the transformers, you
could scale it that way

193
00:07:50,350 --> 00:07:53,560
or in a perceiver, we're
going to be talking about all

194
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:55,370
of the pixels can interact.

195
00:07:55,370 --> 00:07:57,730
So the model can basically
capture these things

196
00:07:57,730 --> 00:07:59,440
and express these
functions much more

197
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:02,860
easily than can be expressed in
things that put locality first.

198
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:06,230

199
00:08:06,230 --> 00:08:08,270
The other sort of
interesting property

200
00:08:08,270 --> 00:08:12,020
of these sorts of architectures
is that position is featurized.

201
00:08:12,020 --> 00:08:13,970
And this basically
means that we're

202
00:08:13,970 --> 00:08:16,760
no longer sort of encoding
the architectural location

203
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:19,820
of something to figure
out where it's located

204
00:08:19,820 --> 00:08:21,350
with respect to the other ones.

205
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:24,800
And this allows the
network to basically use

206
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:27,680
any positional information
that it wants but can also

207
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:30,260
discard it as it prefers.

208
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:31,760
And so this is the
standard way it's

209
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:34,400
done, of course, in the
context of architectures

210
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:37,309
that use Fourier or
sinusoidal-like features.

211
00:08:37,309 --> 00:08:40,365
But there's a lot
of flexibility here.

212
00:08:40,365 --> 00:08:41,740
OK, so now just
thinking in terms

213
00:08:41,740 --> 00:08:44,220
of how ConvNets relate
to transformers sort

214
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:46,630
of at the opposite
end, it may look

215
00:08:46,630 --> 00:08:48,640
like that we have
sort of a scalability

216
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:51,550
versus generality trade off.

217
00:08:51,550 --> 00:08:55,460
And so if we look at ConvNets
the way that they're applied,

218
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:57,490
so typically we can
think about using them

219
00:08:57,490 --> 00:08:59,530
on grid structure data.

220
00:08:59,530 --> 00:09:01,750
There are, of course,
generalizations of convolutions

221
00:09:01,750 --> 00:09:05,350
that work on data sets with
more interesting topology.

222
00:09:05,350 --> 00:09:07,000
But typically we
can think of them

223
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:10,720
as operating on grids
in some sort of space.

224
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:12,850
Whereas, transformers
apply to generic sets.

225
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:16,100
So transformers are more
general from this point of view.

226
00:09:16,100 --> 00:09:18,560
On the other hand, they
scale much, much worse.

227
00:09:18,560 --> 00:09:22,840
So ConvNets are linear both in
the input points, the filter

228
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:25,360
size, and the number of
layers of that architecture,

229
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:28,210
whereas transformers have
this quadratic scaling

230
00:09:28,210 --> 00:09:31,180
and they're still
linear in the depth.

231
00:09:31,180 --> 00:09:32,950
So from this point
of view, what we're

232
00:09:32,950 --> 00:09:35,530
interested in doing in
the perceiver line of work

233
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:39,050
was to scale transformers but to
keep the generality properties.

234
00:09:39,050 --> 00:09:42,970
So we want something that lives
in between these two extremes.

235
00:09:42,970 --> 00:09:46,390
And the way that we
do this is by looking

236
00:09:46,390 --> 00:09:49,120
at self-attention and sort
of modifying it in a way that

237
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:51,610
allows us to scale better.

238
00:09:51,610 --> 00:09:53,860
So to walk through what
self-attention actually

239
00:09:53,860 --> 00:09:56,590
does in sort of
standard transformers,

240
00:09:56,590 --> 00:09:59,860
we take our input array, which
here is written as the number--

241
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:02,050
as the indices, which
is the number of tokens

242
00:10:02,050 --> 00:10:03,692
or the number of
pixels, basically

243
00:10:03,692 --> 00:10:05,650
the number of input units
depending what you're

244
00:10:05,650 --> 00:10:08,270
looking at, and the channels.

245
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:09,680
We have a 1D convolution.

246
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:12,250
So this is O of M
with respect to the Q,

247
00:10:12,250 --> 00:10:15,640
K, and V. We then compute
the attention maps using

248
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:17,290
the output of this operation.

249
00:10:17,290 --> 00:10:18,970
This gives us a
matrix multiply, which

250
00:10:18,970 --> 00:10:21,790
is the source of the
quadratic scaling.

251
00:10:21,790 --> 00:10:23,800
And then finally, we
compute output features

252
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:25,030
with another matrix multiply.

253
00:10:25,030 --> 00:10:29,090

254
00:10:29,090 --> 00:10:31,910
We're already rate limited
here, because for even

255
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:34,110
standard resolution
images, M is quite large.

256
00:10:34,110 --> 00:10:36,770
So it's around 50,000 for
standard ImageNet images,

257
00:10:36,770 --> 00:10:38,030
which again are very small.

258
00:10:38,030 --> 00:10:40,040
So this is something
that just isn't

259
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:43,200
