1010
00:41:09,560 --> 00:41:11,300
is to keep all of
the nice advantages

1011
00:41:11,300 --> 00:41:12,740
of the original perceiver.

1012
00:41:12,740 --> 00:41:15,080
So we have an encoder
that scales linearly.

1013
00:41:15,080 --> 00:41:17,930
We have a processor stage, this
sort of latent self-attention

1014
00:41:17,930 --> 00:41:20,300
that scales independently
of the input size.

1015
00:41:20,300 --> 00:41:23,760
And we now have a decoder
that keeps the decoupling,

1016
00:41:23,760 --> 00:41:26,820
but gives us linear scaling
with respect to output size.

1017
00:41:26,820 --> 00:41:31,340
And so by doing this, we
can now basically apply

1018
00:41:31,340 --> 00:41:35,107
the same approach to
basically dense output tasks.

1019
00:41:35,107 --> 00:41:37,190
And so to give you a sense
of how this works, just

1020
00:41:37,190 --> 00:41:40,400
sort of intuitively, if
we're doing autoencoding

1021
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:43,790
on this image of
puppies, basically what

1022
00:41:43,790 --> 00:41:46,910
we do is we encode,
process, and then to decode,

1023
00:41:46,910 --> 00:41:50,487
we take a query that corresponds
to each of the points.

1024
00:41:50,487 --> 00:41:52,070
And then we pass it
into this decoder.

1025
00:41:52,070 --> 00:41:54,260
So we can query
one of the points,

1026
00:41:54,260 --> 00:41:56,900
we get one pixel, query another
one, we get another one.

1027
00:41:56,900 --> 00:42:00,260
And all the way up, until
we get all 10,000 points.

1028
00:42:00,260 --> 00:42:02,540
And that's how we can do
reconstruction with this.

1029
00:42:02,540 --> 00:42:04,640
And the cool thing about
this is that it opens up

1030
00:42:04,640 --> 00:42:08,600
a bunch of new applications.

1031
00:42:08,600 --> 00:42:10,340
And we can get different
kinds of outputs

1032
00:42:10,340 --> 00:42:12,300
just by changing how
the queries work.

1033
00:42:12,300 --> 00:42:15,020
So if we want to do something
like multimodal autoencoding,

1034
00:42:15,020 --> 00:42:17,150
where we have some of
the outputs or videos,

1035
00:42:17,150 --> 00:42:21,530
we use the same construction
trick to get positions that--

1036
00:42:21,530 --> 00:42:23,900
to get queries that have
the relevant semantics

1037
00:42:23,900 --> 00:42:25,700
for each of the points
that we're decoding.

1038
00:42:25,700 --> 00:42:28,813
And we can do this,
even though, basically,

1039
00:42:28,813 --> 00:42:30,230
the sizes of these
different data,

1040
00:42:30,230 --> 00:42:32,790
so the number of points
they have is quite diverse.

1041
00:42:32,790 --> 00:42:35,000
So in the multimodal
autoencoding experiments

1042
00:42:35,000 --> 00:42:38,060
that we have in this paper,
we do this for video, audio,

1043
00:42:38,060 --> 00:42:39,227
and labels at the same time.

1044
00:42:39,227 --> 00:42:40,685
So that all of them
are just passed

1045
00:42:40,685 --> 00:42:42,800
into their uniform network
and then decoded one

1046
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:44,910
by one in this way.

1047
00:42:44,910 --> 00:42:47,780
But we can also do masked
language modeling now

1048
00:42:47,780 --> 00:42:51,260
by conditioning on the
position in a sequence.

1049
00:42:51,260 --> 00:42:53,180
We can do multitask
classification

1050
00:42:53,180 --> 00:42:56,570
by having basically an index
that gives which task you're

1051
00:42:56,570 --> 00:42:58,610
querying from the network.

1052
00:42:58,610 --> 00:43:00,410
And we can do things
like optical flow

1053
00:43:00,410 --> 00:43:03,860
by passing in input features
as well as the positions.

1054
00:43:03,860 --> 00:43:06,770
And so I'm just going
to just quickly skip

1055
00:43:06,770 --> 00:43:09,530
to a couple of the different--

1056
00:43:09,530 --> 00:43:12,260
I can share these slides with
you, with you all afterwards,

1057
00:43:12,260 --> 00:43:14,480
to look through them.

1058
00:43:14,480 --> 00:43:16,380
Some of these things
are quite cool.

1059
00:43:16,380 --> 00:43:20,120
But just quickly, I want
to talk about language

1060
00:43:20,120 --> 00:43:21,800
and then optical flow.

1061
00:43:21,800 --> 00:43:25,248
So for language,
basically, what we

1062
00:43:25,248 --> 00:43:27,290
wanted to do with this
was to see if we could use

1063
00:43:27,290 --> 00:43:29,570
this to replace tokenization.

1064
00:43:29,570 --> 00:43:33,500
And why might we care about
getting rid of tokenization?

1065
00:43:33,500 --> 00:43:37,310
So we use tokenization primarily
because transformers scale

1066
00:43:37,310 --> 00:43:38,570
poorly with sequence length.

1067
00:43:38,570 --> 00:43:41,540
And tokenizing cuts sequence
length by about a factor of 4.

1068
00:43:41,540 --> 00:43:44,390

1069
00:43:44,390 --> 00:43:46,830
But there are various
problems that arise with this.

1070
00:43:46,830 --> 00:43:52,290
And so why might we care
about removing tokenizers?

1071
00:43:52,290 --> 00:43:58,120
So for one, tokenizers perform
less well on rare words.

1072
00:43:58,120 --> 00:44:01,170
So if you compare the sort of
the byte based decomposition,

1073
00:44:01,170 --> 00:44:02,250
the UTF based--

1074
00:44:02,250 --> 00:44:05,730
UTF-8 encoding of an input--
of an input sequence like this,

1075
00:44:05,730 --> 00:44:07,290
you can see that
there's basically

1076
00:44:07,290 --> 00:44:10,620
a uniform allocation
of points in memory

1077
00:44:10,620 --> 00:44:12,120
to each of the input characters.

1078
00:44:12,120 --> 00:44:13,890
The exception are
diacritics, which

1079
00:44:13,890 --> 00:44:15,870
end up splitting it into two.

1080
00:44:15,870 --> 00:44:18,210
But if you look at the
SentencePiece tokenization,

1081
00:44:18,210 --> 00:44:21,450
so it's learned that
pepper is one token.

1082
00:44:21,450 --> 00:44:25,320
But jalapeno gets split
into five in this case.

1083
00:44:25,320 --> 00:44:27,810
So this basically says it's--

1084
00:44:27,810 --> 00:44:30,090
the amount of capacity
that you allocate

1085
00:44:30,090 --> 00:44:32,400
depends on how rare
the word is, which can

1086
00:44:32,400 --> 00:44:35,680
lead to suboptimal encodings.

1087
00:44:35,680 --> 00:44:37,780
They're also brittle to
subtle perturbations.

1088
00:44:37,780 --> 00:44:41,170
A famous example of this
is that if you enter--

1089
00:44:41,170 --> 00:44:43,960
so if you've ever played
around with GPT-3,

1090
00:44:43,960 --> 00:44:45,340
you'll notice that
the output can

1091
00:44:45,340 --> 00:44:47,530
be quite sensitive
if you add a space

1092
00:44:47,530 --> 00:44:48,970
or omit a space at the end.

1093
00:44:48,970 --> 00:44:50,595
And this basically
is because the space

1094
00:44:50,595 --> 00:44:52,595
can end up being factorized
into different parts

1095
00:44:52,595 --> 00:44:53,440
of the tokenization.

1096
00:44:53,440 --> 00:44:54,250
There are other
problems-- there are

1097
00:44:54,250 --> 00:44:55,917
other things that can
happen there, too,

1098
00:44:55,917 --> 00:44:58,820
but this is one cause of that.

1099
00:44:58,820 --> 00:45:01,550
And finally, tokens don't
transfer across languages.

1100
00:45:01,550 --> 00:45:04,000
So if you wanted to have a
model that, without any tuning,

1101
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:06,730
could be used on many different
languages at the same time,

1102
00:45:06,730 --> 00:45:08,540
tokenizers are a
blocker for this.

1103
00:45:08,540 --> 00:45:11,410
So if we can get rid of them,
it'll simplify the pipeline.

1104
00:45:11,410 --> 00:45:13,240
It'll also make
things less brittle

1105
00:45:13,240 --> 00:45:16,490
and then hopefully lead
to more general models.

1106
00:45:16,490 --> 00:45:18,590
So the way that we do
masked language modeling

1107
00:45:18,590 --> 00:45:20,180
is the same as the
way that I showed

1108
00:45:20,180 --> 00:45:22,820
in that schematic
autoencoding experiment.

1109
00:45:22,820 --> 00:45:26,300
So we mask some fraction
of our inputs, about 15%

1110
00:45:26,300 --> 00:45:28,640
is sort of the
standard magic number.

1111
00:45:28,640 --> 00:45:31,940
We then decode at each of the
positions that are masked.

1112
00:45:31,940 --> 00:45:35,490
And we task the model with
decoding whatever characters

1113
00:45:35,490 --> 00:45:38,225
were masked at those locations.

1114
00:45:38,225 --> 00:45:39,600
And then once we
have this model,

1115
00:45:39,600 --> 00:45:41,270
so this is what we
do for pre-training,

1116
00:45:41,270 --> 00:45:44,510
we can then fine tune it
by replacing the decoder

1117
00:45:44,510 --> 00:45:47,360
with a multitask decoder
that takes in the tasks

1118
00:45:47,360 --> 00:45:50,600
that we're using on the
downstream evaluation

1119
00:45:50,600 --> 00:45:53,960
setting and training the model
to reconstruct the logits

1120
00:45:53,960 --> 00:45:54,815
on a per task basis.

1121
00:45:54,815 --> 00:45:58,330

1122
00:45:58,330 --> 00:46:01,180
OK, so to look at how
this model performs,

1123
00:46:01,180 --> 00:46:04,240
we basically first
compare it to BERT Base.

1124
00:46:04,240 --> 00:46:06,010
So this is just
a solid benchmark

1125
00:46:06,010 --> 00:46:07,390
that we understand very well.

1126
00:46:07,390 --> 00:46:11,380
And first, by looking
at sort of matched

1127
00:46:11,380 --> 00:46:13,120
two models that
have matched FLOPs,

1128
00:46:13,120 --> 00:46:18,238
we can see that Perceiver IO
and BERT Base work on par.

1129
00:46:18,238 --> 00:46:20,030
You see there's a
different trade-off here.

1130
00:46:20,030 --> 00:46:21,700
So to get the same
number of FLOPs,

1131
00:46:21,700 --> 00:46:23,980
basically, we make
Perceiver IO deeper.

1132
00:46:23,980 --> 00:46:26,080
And this ends up giving
it more parameters.

1133
00:46:26,080 --> 00:46:28,480
But on a per FLOPs
basis, it ends up

1134
00:46:28,480 --> 00:46:31,940
performing about the same.

1135
00:46:31,940 --> 00:46:34,570
On the other hand, if we
remove the tokenizer from BERT

1136
00:46:34,570 --> 00:46:36,720
and keep the FLOPs
the same, we see

1137
00:46:36,720 --> 00:46:38,470
that the number of
parameters-- the number

1138
00:46:38,470 --> 00:46:41,140
of parameters and the depth
just drastically fall down.

1139
00:46:41,140 --> 00:46:43,960
And this is because
BERT scales quite poorly

1140
00:46:43,960 --> 00:46:47,140
with sequence length, because
it uses a normal transformer.

1141
00:46:47,140 --> 00:46:50,980
But if we use a perceiver
without the tokenization,

1142
00:46:50,980 --> 00:46:54,250
we can see that we only
get a slight reduction

1143
00:46:54,250 --> 00:46:57,370
in the number of parameters
at the FLOPs count.

1144
00:46:57,370 --> 00:47:00,500
But the performance performs
almost exactly the same.

1145
00:47:00,500 --> 00:47:02,590
So this means that the
perceiver in this setting

1146
00:47:02,590 --> 00:47:04,360
is performing basically
the same with and without

1147
00:47:04,360 --> 00:47:05,200
the tokenization.

1148
00:47:05,200 --> 00:47:06,310
It's learning a
different strategy.

1149
00:47:06,310 --> 00:47:07,700
It's using different parameters.

1150
00:47:07,700 --> 00:47:10,930
But it basically can be brought
to the same performance.

1151
00:47:10,930 --> 00:47:12,820
We can then scale
this more by sort

1152
00:47:12,820 --> 00:47:15,430
of leaning into what happens
in the tokenizer free setting.

1153
00:47:15,430 --> 00:47:17,860
And we see that we can
get a moderate performance

1154
00:47:17,860 --> 00:47:20,307
boost as well.

1155
00:47:20,307 --> 00:47:22,140
I think it's also-- in
the language setting,

1156
00:47:22,140 --> 00:47:24,377
it's also useful to look
at what the attention--

1157
00:47:24,377 --> 00:47:26,210
the attention maps that
are sort of learned.

1158
00:47:26,210 --> 00:47:28,440
And what's being visualized
here are basically,

1159
00:47:28,440 --> 00:47:31,130
for each of the latents, for
some subset of the latents,

1160
00:47:31,130 --> 00:47:33,230
we're looking at where
it's attending to

1161
00:47:33,230 --> 00:47:34,790
in the input sequence.

1162
00:47:34,790 --> 00:47:36,950
And some of these
end up being local,

1163
00:47:36,950 --> 00:47:40,670
so looking at specific
points in the sentence.

1164
00:47:40,670 --> 00:47:42,170
Some of them are
periodic, so they

1165
00:47:42,170 --> 00:47:45,650
look sort of at recurring
points over the sequence.

1166
00:47:45,650 --> 00:47:47,630
And some of them also
look like they pick out

1167
00:47:47,630 --> 00:47:50,100
syntactic features,
which is quite nice.

1168
00:47:50,100 --> 00:47:52,880
So they pick out,
basically, exclamation

1169
00:47:52,880 --> 00:47:55,520
points, or capital letters,
or other punctuation that's

1170
00:47:55,520 --> 00:47:58,010
quite useful and
decodable from right

1171
00:47:58,010 --> 00:47:59,645
at the beginning
of the sequence.

1172
00:47:59,645 --> 00:48:02,300

1173
00:48:02,300 --> 00:48:05,660
We can also basically use
this exact same architecture

1174
00:48:05,660 --> 00:48:07,670
on optical flow.

1175
00:48:07,670 --> 00:48:11,210
And optical flow is basically
an important classical problem

1176
00:48:11,210 --> 00:48:14,510
in computer vision, where given
a pair of frames in a video,

1177
00:48:14,510 --> 00:48:17,520
we want to basically
track all of the points,

1178
00:48:17,520 --> 00:48:20,540
so figure out the motion from
every point from one frame

1179
00:48:20,540 --> 00:48:21,360
to the other.

1180
00:48:21,360 --> 00:48:23,330
And so optical flow
is usually visualized

1181
00:48:23,330 --> 00:48:25,820
using this sort of
colorized images

1182
00:48:25,820 --> 00:48:27,030
that are shown in the bottom.

1183
00:48:27,030 --> 00:48:28,640
And what this
gives you basically

1184
00:48:28,640 --> 00:48:33,430
is a per pixel indication of the
velocity at every single point.

1185
00:48:33,430 --> 00:48:35,690
And so you can see that--

1186
00:48:35,690 --> 00:48:39,600
so the blade the
character here is holding

1187
00:48:39,600 --> 00:48:40,830
is moving to the right.

1188
00:48:40,830 --> 00:48:42,810
Whereas, this
creature behind her

1189
00:48:42,810 --> 00:48:46,770
is sort of moving
as moving downwards.

1190
00:48:46,770 --> 00:48:49,890
So there are a couple of
problems with optical flow

1191
00:48:49,890 --> 00:48:52,900
that make it interesting
to sort of approach.

1192
00:48:52,900 --> 00:48:54,450
So one, it's a dense task.

1193
00:48:54,450 --> 00:48:58,170
And it basically involves
long range correspondences.

1194
00:48:58,170 --> 00:49:00,300
But the standard training
protocol, there's

1195
00:49:00,300 --> 00:49:02,550
basically no large scale
realistic training data,

1196
00:49:02,550 --> 00:49:03,925
just because it's
incredibly hard

1197
00:49:03,925 --> 00:49:06,570
to sort of label all of the
pixels in a real world scene

1198
00:49:06,570 --> 00:49:08,430
and figure out where they go to.

1199
00:49:08,430 --> 00:49:11,130
So the typical way to do this is
to train on some synthetic data

1200
00:49:11,130 --> 00:49:14,850
and then evaluate on
more realistic scenes.

1201
00:49:14,850 --> 00:49:17,880
And optical flow is
also interesting,

1202
00:49:17,880 --> 00:49:20,730
because it's basically
the locus of some

1203
00:49:20,730 --> 00:49:23,460
of the most complicated
visual architectures

1204
00:49:23,460 --> 00:49:24,610
in the literature.

1205
00:49:24,610 --> 00:49:27,480
So the previous state-of-the-art
result here is this method

1206
00:49:27,480 --> 00:49:31,070
called RAFT, which won the best
paper award at ECCV last year.

1207
00:49:31,070 --> 00:49:32,820
And I'm just highlighting
this to give you

1208
00:49:32,820 --> 00:49:35,340
a sense of how much work
people do into sort of hand

1209
00:49:35,340 --> 00:49:37,458
engineering these architectures.

1210
00:49:37,458 --> 00:49:39,750
So this is a very, very
cleverly designed architecture.

1211
00:49:39,750 --> 00:49:43,080
And basically it incorporates
things like global correlation

1212
00:49:43,080 --> 00:49:46,440
volumes that are explicitly
computed at different offsets

1213
00:49:46,440 --> 00:49:49,140
to basically allow the
model to reason about how

1214
00:49:49,140 --> 00:49:50,730
things at different
scales are moving

1215
00:49:50,730 --> 00:49:53,410
with respect to each other.

1216
00:49:53,410 --> 00:49:57,648
It also has local neighborhood
gather operations, as well as

1217
00:49:57,648 --> 00:49:59,190
update blocks to
keep track of what's

1218
00:49:59,190 --> 00:50:02,040
happening within each
specific correlation block.

1219
00:50:02,040 --> 00:50:05,190
And then finally, there's
a flow specific upsampling

1220
00:50:05,190 --> 00:50:07,590
operators that were developed.

1221
00:50:07,590 --> 00:50:10,550
So in contrast to
this, we're basically--

1222
00:50:10,550 --> 00:50:13,660
we wanted to see how well
Perceiver IO would do here.

1223
00:50:13,660 --> 00:50:15,160
And just to give
you a sense of sort

1224
00:50:15,160 --> 00:50:17,150
of what we were expecting
coming into this,

1225
00:50:17,150 --> 00:50:18,627
we thought, well, maybe--

1226
00:50:18,627 --> 00:50:20,960
so Perceiver IO is throwing
a lot of the structure away.

1227
00:50:20,960 --> 00:50:23,060
So we were hoping that we
would get some good results.

1228
00:50:23,060 --> 00:50:24,310
But it would probably overfit.

1229
00:50:24,310 --> 00:50:26,602
And there's this sort of
problem of the domain transfer

1230
00:50:26,602 --> 00:50:27,519
that's happening here.

1231
00:50:27,519 --> 00:50:29,060
But on the other
hand, self-attention

1232
00:50:29,060 --> 00:50:31,070
seems to be a reasonable
way to match this sort

1233
00:50:31,070 --> 00:50:33,020
of correspondence thing.

1234
00:50:33,020 --> 00:50:37,140
What we actually found was
that just by doing the very,

1235
00:50:37,140 --> 00:50:40,910
very simple preprocessing here,
so extracting basically a patch

1236
00:50:40,910 --> 00:50:44,870
around each pixel and then
using the standard Perceiver IO

1237
00:50:44,870 --> 00:50:47,360
architecture, we were able to
get state-of-the-art results

1238
00:50:47,360 --> 00:50:47,860
here.

1239
00:50:47,860 --> 00:50:53,030
And so this is
basically was validation

1240
00:50:53,030 --> 00:50:54,530
of this general
approach of trying

1241
00:50:54,530 --> 00:50:56,750
to have general purpose
architectures that

1242
00:50:56,750 --> 00:50:58,370
would transfer over.

1243
00:50:58,370 --> 00:51:01,340
And so basically
with minimal tuning,

1244
00:51:01,340 --> 00:51:04,100
we are able to get
results that are

1245
00:51:04,100 --> 00:51:08,000
both of the sort of
compelling benchmarks

1246
00:51:08,000 --> 00:51:11,360
on both of the Sintel, the
Sintel evaluation methods,

1247
00:51:11,360 --> 00:51:13,580
and to get comparable
results on KITTI.

1248
00:51:13,580 --> 00:51:15,240
So these are the standard ones.

1249
00:51:15,240 --> 00:51:16,970
And we can also
sort of visualize

1250
00:51:16,970 --> 00:51:20,377
what happens when we apply
this on real world data.

1251
00:51:20,377 --> 00:51:21,710
So there's no ground truth here.

1252
00:51:21,710 --> 00:51:23,810
So you-- we can't
really compare it.

1253
00:51:23,810 --> 00:51:26,720
But it's still useful to sort
of see how it moves around.

1254
00:51:26,720 --> 00:51:28,730
And we can see that
qualitatively, it's

1255
00:51:28,730 --> 00:51:31,310
able to capture a lot
of the fine structure

1256
00:51:31,310 --> 00:51:35,780
and to sort of get the right
motion for the things that

1257
00:51:35,780 --> 00:51:39,450
are very clearly moving
in the specific direction.

1258
00:51:39,450 --> 00:51:41,250
We can also sort of--
it's also, I think,

1259
00:51:41,250 --> 00:51:43,560
informative to look
at what happens--

1260
00:51:43,560 --> 00:51:44,640
how it managed to--

1261
00:51:44,640 --> 00:51:47,550
how it manages to represent
sort of small structure.

1262
00:51:47,550 --> 00:51:50,270
Is this video playing?

1263
00:51:50,270 --> 00:51:52,490
Yeah, we can see it.

1264
00:51:52,490 --> 00:51:53,120
OK, cool.

1265
00:51:53,120 --> 00:51:55,820
So the thing to look at here
is the fine water droplets

1266
00:51:55,820 --> 00:51:58,880
that are sort of flying through
the air as that bird flies by.

1267
00:51:58,880 --> 00:52:02,840
And because we're decoding
at every single output point,

1268
00:52:02,840 --> 00:52:05,180
the architecture is
able to represent those.

1269
00:52:05,180 --> 00:52:08,143
So it's able to capture very,
very fine scale segmentation

1270
00:52:08,143 --> 00:52:09,560
that would be
difficult to capture

1271
00:52:09,560 --> 00:52:11,893
if you had, for example, a
convolutional upsampler here.

1272
00:52:11,893 --> 00:52:15,030

1273
00:52:15,030 --> 00:52:16,910
OK, so I'm just going
to-- also, the light

1274
00:52:16,910 --> 00:52:18,410
might've just gone
off in this room.

1275
00:52:18,410 --> 00:52:21,075

1276
00:52:21,075 --> 00:52:22,380
I'm just going to--

1277
00:52:22,380 --> 00:52:24,425
Also try a task
like gap estimation,

1278
00:52:24,425 --> 00:52:27,570
because Perceiver IO looks
like it can also work

1279
00:52:27,570 --> 00:52:30,900
very well on that modalities.

1280
00:52:30,900 --> 00:52:33,450
Yeah, so we haven't
published anything.

1281
00:52:33,450 --> 00:52:35,160
But some internal
results suggest

1282
00:52:35,160 --> 00:52:37,930
that it works just fine.

1283
00:52:37,930 --> 00:52:39,450
There basically--
there don't seem

1284
00:52:39,450 --> 00:52:41,720
to be-- one of the
surprising things, the things

1285
00:52:41,720 --> 00:52:43,470
that we were a little
bit unsure about was

1286
00:52:43,470 --> 00:52:46,230
how much information was going
to be contained in this latent.

1287
00:52:46,230 --> 00:52:48,720
Because basically, you're
abstracting quite a lot.

1288
00:52:48,720 --> 00:52:51,720
And you're not-- it doesn't have
any 2D structure intrinsically.

1289
00:52:51,720 --> 00:52:54,590
But it does seem
like that it seems

1290
00:52:54,590 --> 00:52:56,340
to be able to represent
things quite well.

1291
00:52:56,340 --> 00:52:59,130
And these sorts of
decoding mechanisms

1292
00:52:59,130 --> 00:53:00,805
do seem to be able to do that.

1293
00:53:00,805 --> 00:53:01,695
Got it, OK.

1294
00:53:01,695 --> 00:53:04,193

1295
00:53:04,193 --> 00:53:05,110
So I'm just going to--

1296
00:53:05,110 --> 00:53:07,240
in the interest of time,
I'm going to skip ahead

1297
00:53:07,240 --> 00:53:08,820
to the conclusion.

1298
00:53:08,820 --> 00:53:11,260
Drew, I had one
question with respect

1299
00:53:11,260 --> 00:53:14,200
to the metrics that you've
shared for the optical flow,

1300
00:53:14,200 --> 00:53:15,100
the number.

1301
00:53:15,100 --> 00:53:20,410
So in the table, it was like
Sinel.final, clean, and KITTI

1302
00:53:20,410 --> 00:53:23,650
were these different data
sets or different metrics,

1303
00:53:23,650 --> 00:53:26,020
same metric for
different sets or these

1304
00:53:26,020 --> 00:53:28,135
are three different metrics?

1305
00:53:28,135 --> 00:53:30,010
Yeah, so these are three
different data sets.

1306
00:53:30,010 --> 00:53:33,430
So Sintel.clean and
Sintel.final are basically two--

1307
00:53:33,430 --> 00:53:37,200
they're two ways of doing the
final rendering for Sintel.

1308
00:53:37,200 --> 00:53:39,790
In all cases, these
methods are trained just

1309
00:53:39,790 --> 00:53:41,530
on the autoflow data set.

1310
00:53:41,530 --> 00:53:44,590
So they're trained on this
sort of general purpose

1311
00:53:44,590 --> 00:53:47,715
kind of wacky synthetic
motion data set.

1312
00:53:47,715 --> 00:53:49,090
And then we're
evaluation-- we're

1313
00:53:49,090 --> 00:53:51,990
evaluating on these different
domains without fine tuning.

1314
00:53:51,990 --> 00:53:52,490
OK.

1315
00:53:52,490 --> 00:53:55,710

1316
00:53:55,710 --> 00:53:58,650
Yeah, the flow has quite--
the data sets are quite small.

1317
00:53:58,650 --> 00:54:01,480
So it's generally even
problematic to fine tune.

1318
00:54:01,480 --> 00:54:01,980
Thank you.

1319
00:54:01,980 --> 00:54:05,730

1320
00:54:05,730 --> 00:54:08,070
OK, so just to summarize--

1321
00:54:08,070 --> 00:54:13,590
What is the ground truth
to find the point error?

1322
00:54:13,590 --> 00:54:16,890
Yeah, so the way
this works is Sintel

1323
00:54:16,890 --> 00:54:21,330
is a computer-- it's basically
a relatively high quality CGI

1324
00:54:21,330 --> 00:54:24,150
movie that was
basically open-source.

1325
00:54:24,150 --> 00:54:26,040
And so they actually
have the ground truth.

1326
00:54:26,040 --> 00:54:28,320
So if you know the ground
truth and the state,

1327
00:54:28,320 --> 00:54:31,560
you can compute the pixel
correspondence from frame

1328
00:54:31,560 --> 00:54:32,190
to frame.

1329
00:54:32,190 --> 00:54:33,690
So that's what's used on Sintel.

1330
00:54:33,690 --> 00:54:35,820
And then KITTI, they
basically have--

1331
00:54:35,820 --> 00:54:39,410
they have a LIDAR sensor
that's used to figure out

1332
00:54:39,410 --> 00:54:40,410
the depth of all points.

1333
00:54:40,410 --> 00:54:43,120
And then they compute
the correspondences.

1334
00:54:43,120 --> 00:54:46,260
So the ground truth is actually
the ground truth optical flow.

1335
00:54:46,260 --> 00:54:49,470
But in general, it's hard
to get dense optical flow.

1336
00:54:49,470 --> 00:54:51,990
It's very expensive
to collect it.

1337
00:54:51,990 --> 00:54:52,680
Great, thanks.

1338
00:54:52,680 --> 00:54:56,790

1339
00:54:56,790 --> 00:54:59,280
OK, so the-- so basically,
just to summarize,

1340
00:54:59,280 --> 00:55:02,790
so the perceivers are
attention-based architectures

1341
00:55:02,790 --> 00:55:05,490
that scale linearly and
work as drop-in replacements

1342
00:55:05,490 --> 00:55:07,540
for transformers on a
variety of settings.

1343
00:55:07,540 --> 00:55:10,950
They also seem to be able
to achieve results that

1344
00:55:10,950 --> 00:55:12,720
are comparable, at
least, in performance

1345
00:55:12,720 --> 00:55:15,482
with models that rely
on 2D convolutions.

1346
00:55:15,482 --> 00:55:17,190
But of course, there
is a trade-off here.

1347
00:55:17,190 --> 00:55:18,997
And so it's good to
be very aware of this,

1348
00:55:18,997 --> 00:55:22,540
of generality versus
speed in specific domains.

1349
00:55:22,540 --> 00:55:24,870
And so as was pointed
out in settings

1350
00:55:24,870 --> 00:55:26,820
where you can use
2D convolutions.

1351
00:55:26,820 --> 00:55:30,300
It's certainly good to
have them in the loop.

1352
00:55:30,300 --> 00:55:32,940
It's basically a
unified architecture

1353
00:55:32,940 --> 00:55:36,420
that allows joint modeling
of different modalities

1354
00:55:36,420 --> 00:55:38,340
of different sizes.

1355
00:55:38,340 --> 00:55:42,210
And basically, overall, it
seems to be a quite flexible

1356
00:55:42,210 --> 00:55:44,533
architecture that's able to
produce a state-of-the-art

1357
00:55:44,533 --> 00:55:46,950
or near state-of-the-art results
on a variety of different

1358
00:55:46,950 --> 00:55:48,030
domains.

1359
00:55:48,030 --> 00:55:51,725
And there's-- in the two papers,
we look at a number of other

1360
00:55:51,725 --> 00:55:53,100
domains that I
didn't talk about,

1361
00:55:53,100 --> 00:55:55,620
including 3D point
cloud modeling,

1362
00:55:55,620 --> 00:55:59,100
replacing the transformer
that's used in the StarCraft,

1363
00:55:59,100 --> 00:56:01,620
in the sort of StarCraft
behavioral cloning agent

1364
00:56:01,620 --> 00:56:03,220
and a couple of others.

1365
00:56:03,220 --> 00:56:05,160
So this does-- we
have a lot of evidence

1366
00:56:05,160 --> 00:56:07,620
that this general approach
seems to work broadly.

1367
00:56:07,620 --> 00:56:09,750
And there's a lot of things
we still haven't tried.

1368
00:56:09,750 --> 00:56:12,750
So we're very interested
in pushing this and always,

1369
00:56:12,750 --> 00:56:16,420
always open for
suggestions and so forth.

1370
00:56:16,420 --> 00:56:19,142
So we're relying on a
large body of related work,

1371
00:56:19,142 --> 00:56:21,600
because we're sort of drawing
from a lot of different areas

1372
00:56:21,600 --> 00:56:22,150
here.

1373
00:56:22,150 --> 00:56:23,820
So here are some highlights.

1374
00:56:23,820 --> 00:56:29,290
And then I just want to thank
my coauthors on this work.

1375
00:56:29,290 --> 00:56:31,000
And of course, I'm
happy to talk more.

1376
00:56:31,000 --> 00:56:33,600

1377
00:56:33,600 --> 00:56:35,758
Thanks, Drew.

1378
00:56:35,758 --> 00:56:36,300
Thanks a lot.

1379
00:56:36,300 --> 00:56:38,670
Thanks, Drew.

1380
00:56:38,670 --> 00:56:41,790
So one question I had
is so what do you think

1381
00:56:41,790 --> 00:56:44,930
is the future of similar models.

1382
00:56:44,930 --> 00:56:47,820
Do you think these
are going to be

1383
00:56:47,820 --> 00:56:51,120
used more in the sort
of transformer community

1384
00:56:51,120 --> 00:56:54,690
to replace ConvNet
and other stuff?

1385
00:56:54,690 --> 00:56:59,310
Yeah, so the-- I think, broadly
speaking, I think it's--

1386
00:56:59,310 --> 00:57:01,080
I think of perceivers
now as sort of--

1387
00:57:01,080 --> 00:57:03,630
because we know how to
adapt them pretty well

1388
00:57:03,630 --> 00:57:05,880
to sort of domains
where we don't have

1389
00:57:05,880 --> 00:57:08,490
a great idea of the right way
to structure an architecture,

1390
00:57:08,490 --> 00:57:09,820
an inductive bias.

1391
00:57:09,820 --> 00:57:13,800
So I think that's one of the
really strong cases for it,

1392
00:57:13,800 --> 00:57:15,540
so settings in which
you don't really

1393
00:57:15,540 --> 00:57:18,420
know what the right way
to structure a problem is.

1394
00:57:18,420 --> 00:57:22,200
I also think these
kinds of approaches

1395
00:57:22,200 --> 00:57:24,090
can be used in
conjunction with ConvNets

1396
00:57:24,090 --> 00:57:28,900
for sort of things that are
as domain agnostic as needed.

1397
00:57:28,900 --> 00:57:33,726
But I think multimodal and
new domains is really--

1398
00:57:33,726 --> 00:57:36,870
that's where these
are obvious choices.

1399
00:57:36,870 --> 00:57:39,460
Also, what do you think are the
current bottlenecks with this?

1400
00:57:39,460 --> 00:57:42,210
And if you don't mind,
if you can disclose,

1401
00:57:42,210 --> 00:57:45,840
what are you working on
towards next with this stuff?

1402
00:57:45,840 --> 00:57:49,410
So I can't-- I can't talk about
too many details about that.

1403
00:57:49,410 --> 00:57:53,230
But a couple of domains--

1404
00:57:53,230 --> 00:57:55,050
so one, we don't really
have a great handle

1405
00:57:55,050 --> 00:57:58,620
on how to use them on
sort of small scale data,

1406
00:57:58,620 --> 00:58:04,410
so data where you don't have
the data to sort of recover

1407
00:58:04,410 --> 00:58:05,470
the inductive bias.

1408
00:58:05,470 --> 00:58:08,170
So this is, I think, a
really important area.

1409
00:58:08,170 --> 00:58:10,603
The other thing that we haven't
sort of talked about here,

1410
00:58:10,603 --> 00:58:13,020
but you could probably imagine
that we'd be thinking about

1411
00:58:13,020 --> 00:58:15,187
would be how to train on
multiple modalities or sort

1412
00:58:15,187 --> 00:58:17,140
of multiple things
at the same time.

1413
00:58:17,140 --> 00:58:19,170
So right now, all of
these architectures

1414
00:58:19,170 --> 00:58:21,690
are sort of trained
in isolation.

1415
00:58:21,690 --> 00:58:24,330
But there are a lot
of opportunities

1416
00:58:24,330 --> 00:58:27,240
for sort of figuring out how to
pose problems together and use

1417
00:58:27,240 --> 00:58:29,500
a single architecture
on all of them.

1418
00:58:29,500 --> 00:58:30,000
Got it.

1419
00:58:30,000 --> 00:58:32,140
Also, I'm sure if you've
tried, but can you also

1420
00:58:32,140 --> 00:58:34,910
use this for tabular data?

1421
00:58:34,910 --> 00:58:38,330
Yeah, so effectively,
the architecture

1422
00:58:38,330 --> 00:58:41,220
treats any input
data as tabular data.

1423
00:58:41,220 --> 00:58:45,600
So I think that's exactly the
right way to think about it.

1424
00:58:45,600 --> 00:58:46,447
Sounds good.

1425
00:58:46,447 --> 00:58:47,280
Thanks for the talk.

1426
00:58:47,280 --> 00:58:50,860
I will open mic general
questions for the students.

1427
00:58:50,860 --> 00:58:53,150
So stop the recording.

1428
00:58:53,150 --> 00:58:58,000

